WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Start with an effective, proper inclusion h ⊂ g
of compact real Lie algebras.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.042
q = g/h is the entire adjoint complement.
No physical homogeneous-space hypothesis is assumed.

3
00:00:11.042 --> 00:00:16.958
First input: End_h(q) ≅ C.
The real h-module q is irreducible of complex type.

4
00:00:16.958 --> 00:00:22.875
q_C = V ⊕ V*: two inequivalent irreducible modules.
The two complex orientations exchange V and V*.

5
00:00:22.875 --> 00:00:29.417
The OTHER INPUT, alongside complex type:
k = Rad(c_V) must be nonzero.

6
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:35.958
c_V(k, h, h) = 0: one radical input kills the cubic
for all other h inputs, including every mixed insertion.

7
00:00:35.958 --> 00:00:43.000
BOTH inputs imply: g simple; h semisimple and maximal.
The long paper's all-rank reduction leaves six candidates.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.042
Five cubic radicals vanish. Only E8 ⊃ E6 + A2 survives,
with Rad(c_V) = e6.

9
00:00:50.042 --> 00:00:56.958
V = 27 ⊗ 3. Under Spin(10), each 27 = 16 ⊕ 10 ⊕ 1.
The representation carries a factor of three.

10
00:00:56.958 --> 00:01:03.917
Under SU(5): 27 = (10 ⊕ 5̄) ⊕ (5 ⊕ 5̄) ⊕ 2·1.
One chiral family class, a conjugate pair and two singlets.

11
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:10.667
After restriction to the Standard Model group,
χ(V) = 3χ(F_SM): three minimal family classes.

12
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:17.417
Conjugate pairs and singlets have zero net chiral class.
This is the mathematical net chirality of the restricted module.

13
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:22.042
The dual orientation reverses the sign:
χ(V*) = −3χ(F_SM).
